{"name":"Slice of Heaven","hex":"#0022ee","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"slice","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["That which is thin and broad.","A thin, broad piece cut off."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cut into slices.","To cut with an edge using a drawing motion."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/slice"},{"term":"heaven","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The sky, specifically:\n The distant sky in which the sun, moon, and stars appear or move; the firmament; the celestial spheres.\n\n The near sky in which weather, flying animals, etc. appear;  the atmosphere; the climate.\n\n A model displaying the movement of the celestial bodies, an orrery.","The distant sky in which the sun, moon, and stars appear or move; the firmament; the celestial spheres."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To transport to the abode of God, the gods, or the blessed.","To beatify, enchant, or please greatly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/heaven"}],"entries":[{"title":"Slice of Heaven","blocks":[{"text":"\"Slice of Heaven\" is a single by New Zealand singer-songwriter Dave Dobbyn with the band Herbs, released in 1986 on the soundtrack of the animated motion picture, Footrot Flats: The Dog's Tail Tale. The single reached No. 1 on the New Zealand Singles Chart for eight weeks and on the Australian Singles Chart for four weeks. It appears on Dobbyn's 1988 album, Loyal."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/56/SliceOfHeaven.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slice_of_Heaven"},{"title":"Heaven","blocks":[{"text":"Heaven, or the Heavens, is a common religious cosmological or supernatural place where beings such as deities, angels, souls, saints, or venerated ancestors are said to originate, be enthroned, or reside. According to the beliefs of some religions, heavenly beings can descend to Earth or incarnate and earthly beings can ascend to Heaven in the afterlife or, in exceptional cases, enter Heaven without dying."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Paradiso_Canto_31_%28148200393%29.jpg/330px-Paradiso_Canto_31_%28148200393%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heaven"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/Beowulf_-_heofones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/Beowulf_-_heofones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"\"heofones\", an ancient Anglo-Saxon word for heavens in Beowulf","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeowulf_-_heofones.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Ruins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg/500px-Ruins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Ruins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg/1280px-Ruins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Ruins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg/500px-Ruins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ruins of the Ekur temple in Nippur, believed by the ancient Mesopotamians to be the \"Dur-an-ki\", the \"mooring rope\" of heaven and earth[6][7]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARuins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Francesco_Botticini_-_The_Assumption_of_the_Virgin.jpg/500px-Francesco_Botticini_-_The_Assumption_of_the_Virgin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://t